===Etymology 1===
{{wp}}
From {{inh|en|enm|licence}}, {{m|enm|licens}}, {{m|enm|lisence}}, {{m|enm|lissens}}, {{m|enm|licance}}, from {{der|en|fro|licence}}, from {{der|en|la|licentia||license}}, from {{m|la|licens}}, present participle of {{m|la|licere||to be allowed, be allowable}}; compare {{m|la|linquere}}, {{cog|grc|λείπω||leave}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun|~}} {{tlb|en|American spelling}}

# A [[legal]] [[document]] giving [[official]] [[permission]] to do something; a [[permit]].
#* {{quote-av|en|year=1970|actor=John Cleese|title=Monty Python's Flying Circus|season=2|number=10|time=0:1:15|text=Hello. I would like to buy a fish '''licence''' please.}}
# The legal [[term]]s under which a person is [[allow]]ed to use a product, especially [[software]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1986|author=Thomas Smedinghoff|title=The Legal Guide to Developing, Protecting, and Marketing Software|url=https://www.worldcat.org/title/legal-guide-to-developing-protecting-and-marketing-software-dealing-with-problems-raised-by-customers-competitors-and-employees/oclc/12369834|page=166|text=Thus, while the '''license''' will grant the user the right to use the software, a major concern is the scope of that use. For example, will the user be granted the right to copy, modify, or transfer the software?}}
# [[freedom|Freedom]] to [[deviate]] deliberately from normally applicable [[rule]]s or [[practice]]s (especially in [[behaviour]] or [[speech]]).
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2012|author=Chris Seepe|title=The Conspiracy to Assassinate Jesus Christ|url=https://www.worldcat.org/title/conspiracy-to-assassinate-jesus-christ/oclc/885339746|page=5|text=In some instances, the author took '''license''' to include events which never happened, or to purposely create events which may run in the face of popular conjecture if the author felt it would help the story along.}}
# [[excessive|Excessive]] freedom; [[lack]] of [[due]] [[restraint]].
#* {{RQ:Tennyson In Memoriam|XXVII|44|I envy not the beast that takes<br>⁠His '''license''' in the field of time,<br>⁠Unfetter’d by the sense of crime,<br>To whom a conscience never wakes; {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1918|author=James George Frazer|title=Folk-Lore In The Old Testament|volume=1|page=497|text=Indeed the custom [''jus primae noctis''], far from originating in '''licence''', appears on the contrary to have taken its rise in an austere practice of chastity which was inculcated by the early Christian Church.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1936|author=w:Will Durant; w:Ariel Durant|title=The Story of Civilization|page=520|passage=When liberty becomes '''license''' dictatorship is near.}}
# {{ellipsis of|en|driver's license}}.
#: {{ux|en|In order to enter the building, I need to show my '''license'''.}}

=====Usage notes=====
* In British English, Canadian English, Australian English, Irish English, South African English and New Zealand English the noun is spelt {{m|en||licence}} and the verb is {{m|en||license}}.
* The spelling {{m|en||licence}} is not used for either part of speech in the United States.

